I have gathered everything needed. Here is my thorough report on the owner/identity duplicate-detection logic.

# Owner / Identity Duplicate-Detection Logic

Important overall finding up front: there are TWO conflicting definitions of `identityKey` in this repo. The production code and the OpenAPI spec define it as a plaintext pipe-joined string; the acceptance test expects a completely different "v2" definition (SHA-256 hex over telephone/email/soundex). No soundex code exists anywhere. Details below.

---

## 1. identityKey computation (SHA-256 hex, normalizedTelephone, lowerEmail, soundex, lastName)

Two divergent definitions exist:

### (a) Actual production implementation — plaintext, NOT hashed, NO soundex
`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Owner.java` lines 366-379:
```java
@Transient
public String getIdentityKey() {
    return (this.telephone == null ? "" : this.telephone)
        + "|" + (this.email == null ? "" : this.email)
        + "|" + (this.householdId == null ? "" : this.householdId);
}
```
So the real key is `telephone | email | householdId`, joined with `'|'`, in plaintext. It does not hash, does not lowercase within this method (email is lowered by the setter, see item 7), and uses `householdId`, not `soundex(lastName)`.

The email lowercasing happens in the setter, `Owner.java` lines 177-179:
```java
public void setEmail(String email) {
    this.email = email == null ? null : email.toLowerCase();
}
```

The OpenAPI spec agrees with the production code (not the test), `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/openapi.yml` lines 2379-2389:
```yaml
identityKey:
  ...
  '<normalizedTelephone>|<email or empty>|<householdId or empty>'.
  ... a create is rejected with 409 on an exact full-key match ...
  example: '+61412345678||A1B2C3D4E5F6A7B8'
```

The existence check is in `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/service/ClinicServiceImpl.java` lines 345-354:
```java
public boolean existsOwnerWithIdentityKey(String identityKey) throws DataAccessException {
    if (identityKey == null) {
        return false;
    }
    return ownerRepository.findAll().stream()
        .filter(existing -> !existing.isDeleted())
        .anyMatch(existing -> identityKey.equals(existing.getIdentityKey()));
}
```

### (b) Acceptance-test expectation — "identity-key-v2", SHA-256 hex + soundex
`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceTest.java` lines 9-19:
```java
/** identity-key-v2: identityKey = SHA-256 hex over (normalizedTelephone | lowerEmail |
 * soundex(lastName)); duplicate detection (409) uses it. ... 64-hex shape. */
...
@Test
void coreIdentityKeyIsSha256Hex() throws Exception {
    JsonNode r = fetchOwner(createOwnerOk(structuredOwner()));
    String key = r.get("identityKey").asText();
    assertTrue(key.matches("[0-9a-f]{64}"), key);
}
```
This test asserts a 64-char lowercase hex value produced from `normalizedTelephone | lowerEmail | soundex(lastName)`. The current `getIdentityKey()` returns a plaintext pipe string (e.g. `+61412345678||A1B2...`), which will NOT match `[0-9a-f]{64}`, and there is no soundex. This looks like an intended-but-unimplemented spec, or a test written against a future implementation.

Note the related but separate SHA-256 helpers used for the customer code and household id (item 2), `ClinicServiceImpl.java` `hash8(...)` lines 318-338 and `sha256UpperHex(...)` lines 540-557 — these are the only actual SHA-256 usages in production code, and they operate on `telephone + lastName` and `normalizedLastName + '|' + postcode` respectively, uppercase-hex truncated, not on the identity key.

---

## 2. householdId computation and the "household-duplicate" 409 rejection

### householdId computation
`ClinicServiceImpl.java` lines 430-451:
```java
public String computeHouseholdId(Owner owner) throws DataAccessException {
    if (owner == null) return null;
    String normalizedLastName = normalizeHouseholdField(owner.getLastName());
    String postcode = owner.getPostcode();
    if (normalizedLastName == null || normalizedLastName.isBlank()
        || postcode == null || postcode.isBlank()) {
        return null;
    }
    return sha256UpperHex(normalizedLastName + "|" + postcode, 12);
}
```
Household id = first 12 upper-hex chars of SHA-256 over `normalizedLastName + "|" + postcode`. The `sha256UpperHex` helper is at lines 540-557. The field is stored on the entity: `Owner.java` line 88-89 (`@Column(name="household_id") private String householdId;`), getter/setter lines 231-237.

### "existsOwnerInHousehold" check
`ClinicServiceImpl.java` lines 459-468:
```java
public boolean existsOwnerInHousehold(String householdId) throws DataAccessException {
    if (householdId == null || householdId.isBlank()) return false;
    return ownerRepository.findAll().stream()
        .filter(existing -> !existing.isDeleted())
        .anyMatch(existing -> householdId.equals(existing.getHouseholdId()));
}
```

### Important caveat re "household-duplicate 409"
Despite the term "household-duplicate", the controller does NOT return 409 for `existsOwnerInHousehold`. In `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`, the `householdExists` boolean is used only to (a) compute the membership-level cap and (b) SUPPRESS the possible-duplicate soft-match flag — it does not itself trigger a 409. See `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java` lines 145-165:
```java
owner.setHouseholdId(this.clinicService.computeHouseholdId(owner));
boolean householdExists = this.clinicService.existsOwnerInHousehold(owner.getHouseholdId());
owner.setMembershipLevelCap(this.clinicService.computeMembershipLevelCap(owner.getHouseholdId()));
if (this.clinicService.existsOwnerWithIdentityKey(owner.getIdentityKey())) {
    return new ResponseEntity<>(HttpStatus.CONFLICT);   // the only identity 409
}
Integer possibleDuplicateOf = householdExists ? null
    : this.clinicService.findPossibleDuplicateOwnerId(owner);
```
So the only duplicate-based 409 is the full identity-key match. A shared household alone is allowed (because the household id is part of the identity key, two same-household owners still differ if telephone or email differ). There is no dedicated "household-duplicate" rejection path in the code.

Other household helpers in `ClinicServiceImpl.java`: `countOwnersByHouseholdId` (471-484), `findOwnersByHouseholdId` (486-502), `computeMembershipLevelCap` (504-526), and the normalizer `normalizeHouseholdField` (528-538).

---

## 3. Email-domain blocklist logic

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/validation/EmailDomainValidator.java` (whole file, lines 1-28):
```java
private static final Set<String> BLOCKED_DOMAINS =
    Set.of("mailinator.com", "tempmail.com", "guerrillamail.com");

@Override
public boolean isValid(String email, ConstraintValidatorContext context) {
    if (email == null || email.isBlank()) return true;
    int at = email.lastIndexOf('@');
    if (at < 0 || at == email.length() - 1) return true;
    String domain = email.substring(at + 1).toLowerCase(Locale.ROOT);
    return !BLOCKED_DOMAINS.contains(domain);
}
```
The annotation is `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/validation/EmailDomainValidation.java` lines 14-26 (field-level constraint, message lists the three blocked domains). A blocked domain fails bean validation, yielding a 400 (not 409).

---

## 4. "Soft match" (possible duplicate) logic

A soft match = an owner sharing an existing owner's lastName (case-insensitive) AND postcode but with a DIFFERENT telephone. Recorded via two persisted fields: `possibleDuplicate` (Boolean flag) and `possibleDuplicateOf` (Integer id of the matched owner).

Detection in `ClinicServiceImpl.java` lines 356-382:
```java
public Integer findPossibleDuplicateOwnerId(Owner owner) throws DataAccessException {
    if (owner == null || owner.getLastName() == null || owner.getPostcode() == null) return null;
    String lastName = owner.getLastName();
    String postcode = owner.getPostcode();
    String telephone = owner.getTelephone();
    return ownerRepository.findAll().stream()
        .filter(existing -> !existing.isDeleted())
        .filter(existing -> lastName.equalsIgnoreCase(existing.getLastName())
            && postcode.equals(existing.getPostcode())
            && !Objects.equals(telephone, existing.getTelephone()))
        .map(Owner::getId)
        .filter(Objects::nonNull)
        .min(Comparator.naturalOrder())
        .orElse(null);   // lowest matching id, or null
}
```

Flag assignment (and suppression when joining an existing household) in `OwnerRestControllerV1.java` lines 159-165 (quoted in item 2).

Entity fields/getters in `Owner.java`: declarations lines 94-98, getters/setters `getPossibleDuplicate`/`setPossibleDuplicate` lines 260-271 and `getPossibleDuplicateOf`/`setPossibleDuplicateOf` lines 273-283. OpenAPI fields at `openapi.yml` lines 2327 (`possibleDuplicate`) and 2337 (`possibleDuplicateOf`).

---

## 5. "Deleted" owner flag handling

Field: `Owner.java` lines 103-104 (`@Column(name="deleted") private boolean deleted;`). Getter/setter `isDeleted`/`setDeleted` lines 353-364 (Javadoc: deleted owners are ignored by the create endpoint's duplicate and identity checks).

Soft-delete write path: `ClinicServiceImpl.deleteOwner` lines 153-160:
```java
public void deleteOwner(Owner owner) throws DataAccessException {
    owner.setDeleted(true);
    ownerRepository.save(owner);
}
```
Invoked from `OwnerRestControllerV1.deleteOwner` lines 202-212 (returns 204 NO_CONTENT).

Deleted owners are excluded via `.filter(existing -> !existing.isDeleted())` in the identity check (line 352), soft-match check (line 374), `existsOwnerInHousehold` (line 466), and `findOwnersByHouseholdId` (line 499). Note: `countOwnersByHouseholdId` (line 482) does NOT exclude deleted owners — an asymmetry worth flagging. OpenAPI field at `openapi.yml` lines 2317-2321.

---

## 6. Where 409 (CONFLICT / duplicate) errors are thrown

All in `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`, inside `addOwner`:
- Line 142-144: per-city capacity — `if (countOwnersByCity(...) >= 50) return CONFLICT;` (a capacity limit, not a duplicate).
- Line 156-158: identity-key duplicate — `if (existsOwnerWithIdentityKey(owner.getIdentityKey())) return CONFLICT;` (the actual duplicate rejection).

Those are the only two `HttpStatus.CONFLICT` sites in the codebase. (Related non-409 rejections in the same method: line 139-140 returns 429 TOO_MANY_REQUESTS for the per-day cap.)

---

## 7. Normalization helpers for telephone and email

### Telephone
`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/validation/TelephoneNormalizer.java`:
- `toE164(String raw)` lines 35-54 — strips non-digits, keeps leading `+`, else assumes AU `+61` and drops a single leading `0`; enforces 8-15 total digits and per-country national length.
- `hasValidNationalLength` lines 66-74 (`+61` => 9 national digits, `+1` => 10).
- `isValid` lines 81-83; `toDisplay` lines 95-109; `countryCodeLength` lines 119-127.

Wired in via MapStruct: `OwnerMapper.normalizeTelephone` lines 265-268 (`TelephoneNormalizer.toE164`), mapped on `toOwner(OwnerFieldsDto)` at line 231; also used in `OwnerRestControllerV1.updateOwner` line 195. Telephone display for output via `OwnerMapper.formatTelephoneDisplay` lines 222-225. The E.164 pattern is also enforced on the entity: `Owner.java` line 70. Bean-validation classes: `TelephoneValidation.java` / `TelephoneValidator.java` in the validation package.

### Email
No dedicated normalizer class; normalization is just lowercasing in the setter `Owner.setEmail` lines 177-179 (quoted in item 1). Domain-level blocklist normalization (lowercasing the domain) is in `EmailDomainValidator` line 25.

### Household field (related)
`ClinicServiceImpl.normalizeHouseholdField` lines 528-538 — trims, collapses whitespace, lowercases; used for householdId.

---

## 8. Soundex implementation

There is NO soundex implementation anywhere in the repository. The only occurrence of the word "soundex" is the descriptive comment in the acceptance test, `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceTest.java` lines 9-11 ("identityKey = SHA-256 hex over (normalizedTelephone | lowerEmail | soundex(lastName))"). No production class computes soundex; `pom.xml` and other resources contain no soundex/commons-codec reference. This is a gap between the stated "identity-key-v2" spec and the actual code (which uses `householdId`, not `soundex(lastName)`, and does not hash the identity key at all).

---

## Relevant test files

There are no dedicated unit tests for `ClinicServiceImpl`, the mapper, or the validators. The only tests exercising this logic are the acceptance (integration) tests:

- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceTest.java` — `coreIdentityKeyIsSha256Hex` (64-hex identityKey, lines 14-19) and `coreRejectsDuplicateIdentity` (identical identity -> 409, lines 21-28). Integration-style via MockMvc.
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceBase.java` — `@SpringBootTest @AutoConfigureMockMvc @Transactional` base; helpers relevant here: `uniqueEmail` (106-108), `uniqueTelephone` (98-104), `structuredOwner` (136-142), `createOwner`/`createOwnerOk`/`createOwnerWithKey` (155-168), `deleteOwner` soft-delete helper (193-197), `sha256hex`/`shaHex` (281-299), region/postcode reference tables (228-237).
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AuditLogCapture.java` — audit-log capture support (tangential; supports the audit assertions, not the duplicate logic directly).

If dedicated unit coverage for `existsOwnerWithIdentityKey`, `computeHouseholdId`, `findPossibleDuplicateOwnerId`, `TelephoneNormalizer`, or `EmailDomainValidator` is expected, it does not currently exist in the repo.

